WEBVTT

NOTE
The walkthrough has no sound: these are the captions shown on screen, with their timings.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.055
UnderCover: a team of AI agents that settles routine travel insurance claims, while claims experts make the judgement calls.

00:00:05.055 --> 00:00:10.078
Three apps: a portal for customers, a dashboard for claims handlers, and an AI control plane for engineers.

00:00:10.078 --> 00:00:11.391
It starts with the code.

00:00:11.396 --> 00:00:15.374
One repo: the agents, API, MCP servers and three web apps in apps/, Terraform in infra/, decisions and the threat model in docs/.

00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:21.661
Every PR runs secret, dependency, IaC and code scans, CodeQL, unit tests, end-to-end tests and the agent's exam.

00:00:21.661 --> 00:00:27.936
On AWS, the agents run on Bedrock AgentCore in private subnets, reach tools only through an MCP gateway under Cedar policy, and call Claude on Bedrock.

00:00:27.936 --> 00:00:31.952
A STRIDE threat model came before the code: trust boundaries, each threat numbered where it lands, with the control that stops it.

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:35.468
Top risk, R1: prompt injection in a claim or receipt. A separate check reads every claim before any agent does.

00:00:35.475 --> 00:00:40.715
Oliver's bag didn't arrive in Queenstown. He signs in to the customer portal.

00:00:40.715 --> 00:00:50.810
He tells Wattle, the portal's AI assistant, what happened, in his own words.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:55.554
Wattle asks only for what's missing. Oliver adds the shop's receipt and the airline's delay report.

00:00:55.554 --> 00:01:00.411
Wattle drafts the claim from his words and files. Oliver checks it and sends it.

00:01:00.411 --> 00:01:02.726
Sent in about a minute: no forms. The claims agent picks it up straight away.

00:01:02.735 --> 00:01:06.283
Dana, a senior claims handler, starts from Today: what needs her, worked out from the queue.

00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:10.051
The agent assessed Oliver's claim: payable at $174.87, and every check cites the policy wording, the claims manual or a document.

00:01:10.051 --> 00:01:14.091
But it's his third claim in 12 months. A rule in the claims manual sends it to a person, whatever its merits, and nothing tips Oliver off.

00:01:14.091 --> 00:01:22.675
Dana asks the Copilot. It reads the claim, its documents and his history, and answers with sources.

00:01:22.675 --> 00:01:30.444
Dana makes the call. Code pays it, within her limit, and the decision is on the record: who, when and why.

00:01:30.444 --> 00:01:33.505
Paid. Oliver sees the decision on his claim, and the audit log keeps it.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:41.423
The AI control plane. Nine Claude agents, each with its model, its work and its cost, beside a live feed of every tool call and hand-off.

00:01:41.423 --> 00:01:45.764
Each agent opens its tools, what it reads, its worst case if tricked, and what stops it. The claim's path runs left to right.

00:01:45.768 --> 00:01:56.456
Oliver's claim, CLM-1048, step by step: screened for hidden instructions first, then his policy, the documents specialist and tools over MCP.

00:01:56.456 --> 00:02:02.121
Every model call with its tokens, time and cost. Every tool took the claim from the session, never from the model.

00:02:02.124 --> 00:02:09.345
Every agent version sits an exam: claims experts' decisions on held-out claims, scored on accuracy, safety and cost.

00:02:09.345 --> 00:02:15.761
Models are compared on the same claims, so the cheapest one that meets the targets runs.

00:02:15.761 --> 00:02:21.551
An improver agent proposes a change and an engineer approves it, but the exam and the red team decide. This round's proposal was rolled back: it settled fewer claims without a person.

00:02:21.554 --> 00:02:25.365
Would we stop an attack? Worked out in code from the latest red-team run, four threat frameworks and what fired on live claims.

00:02:25.365 --> 00:02:36.269
Every risk in OWASP's LLM and Agentic Top 10, the MCP Top 10 and CSA MAESTRO, mapped to its control and the attacks that test it.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:44.471
The red team attacks through the real portal: instructions hidden in receipts, forged approvals, a walk toward a payout promise.

00:02:44.471 --> 00:02:48.084
On live claims, the guardrails show what they caught, each one opening its claim's trace.

00:02:48.087 --> 00:02:52.289
Under a cent of Claude a claim, against a daily budget that stops the agent on its own.

00:02:52.289 --> 00:02:56.278
And one click stops the agent or pauses every payout, with the reason on the record.
